Some people choose naturalization and permanent residence, but the nature is completely different, so be careful about which application you make after understanding how they differ so that you will not regret it later. Let's choose.
If you apply for naturalization, you can acquire Japanese nationality (Japanese passport) and live in Japan as a Japanese citizen.
However, since Japan does not accept multinationality, you will lose your nationality.
Japan has the highest number of countries in the world that can be reached without a visa (as of September 2021).
Of course, when you return to your home country, you can return without a visa because you still have the nationality of your home country.
But, Disadvantages of losing the nationality of your own country Please consider carefully whether to apply for naturalization or permanent residence.
Naturalization and permanent residence also have different requirements when applying.
In addition, children of Japanese nationals who have an address in Japan and those who have lost Japanese nationality and who require an address in Japan were underage at the time of adopting a Japanese national, regardless of the number of years of residence. A person can apply for naturalization if he / she has an address in Japan for one year or more.
Residents and refugee status recipients will be able to apply if they have lived in Japan for at least XNUMX years.
Marriage life with reality is basically required to live together.
If the payment deadline for health insurance has passed even once, it may not be permitted at that time.
In addition, whether or not you can live a stable life depends on the area where the applicant lives.
Even if you have at least enough income to live a stable life, there is a good chance that you will get a permit.
Unlike naturalization applications, permanent residence applications are subject to strict examination of livelihood requirements.
In recent years, this amount has been emphasized, so if it is less than 300 million yen, you should be prepared for disapproval.
Naturalization is basically irrevocable once it is allowed.
Therefore, once naturalization is approved, you will not lose your Japanese nationality unless you naturalize again in another country.
Permanent residence has a considerable variation in the examination period until the results are obtained.
However, on average, results are often obtained in about XNUMX months.
As you can see from the reading so far, the hurdles for applying for naturalization are lower, so there are many people who do not meet the requirements for permanent residence but can apply for naturalization.
There will be some procedures that you will have to do every time you return to your country.
Think carefully about whether naturalization or permanent residence is right for you.
