Another cause of cough in children may be inflammation of the ENT organs (nose, paranasal sinuses, pharynx), the presence of adenoids (an increase in the pharyngeal tonsil).
A sudden coughing in an infant may be a signal of inhaling a foreign body into the trachea and bronchi, which poses a serious threat to the child's life and requires immediate medical intervention.
The cause of dry cough, in which the amount of sputum is insignificant, is usually inflammation of the upper respiratory tract (pharynx, larynx, trachea). Such a cough can be intrusive and painful.
More liquid sputum clears easier - and with a superficial cough, while thicker and more viscous require more effort; cough in this case becomes deeper. After spitting out, the attack of coughing usually stops, and after its accumulation begins again.
