Discharge from the eyes is a common disorder in newborns that is usually associated with obstruction of the lacrimal duct. Allocations arising along with other symptoms in the eye area are a sign of infection.
A common cause is obstruction of the lacrimal duct. Doctors sometimes call this condition "dacryostenosis," or "obstruction of the nasopharyngeal duct."
A newborn with a blocked lacrimal canal is allowed to treat independently at home, following the recommendations of the attending physician.
To remove the discharge, it is necessary to dip a clean piece of gauze or soft cloth in warm water, and then gently rub the corner of the eye. If a blocked lacrimal canal affects both eyes, use new tissue or gauze.
Make 2 or 3 short pressure with your finger along the side of the nose. they must be tender but firm;
if the nose of the newborn becomes red or swollen, stop massage immediately and consult a doctor.
In newborns, blocked lacrimal ducts tend to open independently within a few months after birth. However, if the blockage does not disappear by the 1 year of life, the doctor may recommend medical treatment.
Sometimes a doctor may also insert a small tube or stent into the canal so that it remains open. Before performing this procedure, the doctor may give the child anesthetic eye drops.
It is important to seek medical attention if the lacrimal canal of the baby remains blocked after 6 – 8 months.
red, sore, or swollen eyes;
Redness, swelling, or soreness in the eye area may indicate eye infection. If a child has these symptoms, you should immediately visit a doctor.
