Does brain chemistry affect anxiety disorders?
People with multiple sclerosis suffer the most from anxiety disorders.
Scientists at the University of Cambridge found during the study that women are much more likely to suffer from depressive disorders than men. Doctors published the results of the study in the journal Brain and Behavior.
In North America, almost 8 of 100 people suffer from the so-called "anxiety and depressive disorders." In East Asia, for example, less than 3 out of every 100 people are anxious.
Some people seem to think that fear is part of the personality. Excessive care, anxiety, and avoiding potentially stressful situations, such as social gatherings, characterize anxiety and depressive disorders.
About 4 of 100 people suffer from depressive disorders. It was noticeable that women suffer about twice as often as men, doctors explain.
People with health problems often suffer from anxiety and depressive disorders.
People with other health problems also suffer more from anxiety disorders. Researchers explain that about 70% of people with anxiety disorders also suffer from physical illnesses.
Among people with heart disease, approximately 11% had anxiety disorders in Western countries, especially women. People with multiple sclerosis suffer the most, and up to 32% are depressed.
Conversational therapy, medications, and lifestyle changes can help patients. Different cultures deal with fears differently.
