Nutritional Supplements in Frozen Meat, Fish, Sausages or Cheeses suppress the immune system. Exposure to specific substances may decrease the effectiveness of the flu vaccine.
A recent study by Michigan State University showed that tert-butyl hydroquinone supplementation inhibits the body's ability to fight the flu. The research results were presented on April 9 at a meeting of the American Society of Pharmacology in the United States.
Flu-related breathing problems claim the lives of up to 700 thousands of people every year. The relationship may explain why seasonal flu continues to be a serious global health risk.
In a model experiment in mice, tert-butylhydroquinone suppressed the function of various immune cells. Researchers explain that this ultimately led to more serious symptoms after reinfection with an influenza infection.
The release of protein compounds has been enhanced in different laboratory models. However, further research is needed to determine whether protein activation is actually responsible for weakening immunity.
Later, when the mice were again infected with a different but related strain of influenza, the animals were sick longer and lost weight. Supplement interferes with the “immune memory”, therefore, complicates the treatment of influenza.
Tert-butylhydroquinone is available with E319 labeling. In low concentrations, it protects cells from destruction, and in high ones it destroys them.
