Many studies show that eating soy reduces the risk of developing cancer, such as breast and prostate. Components of soybeans such as lunazin and isoflavones suppress cancer cells and “work” as a prophylactic.
Osteoporosis is characterized by a decrease in bone tissue density and an increased probability of fractures, especially in older women. Eating soybeans minimizes the risk of developing this ailment in women with menopause.
The menopause period is characterized by symptoms such as mood swings, hot flashes, excessive sweating, which is the result of a decrease in estrogen levels. Interesting fact: Asian women are less likely to suffer from unpleasant symptoms of menopause than women who live in the West.
An experiment was even conducted with the participation of 36 women who had no menstruation for six months. The results were surprising: in women who took 6 g of black soybean powder daily, the menstrual cycle normalized and ovulation improved.
Soybeans are pure protein, and when there is enough protein in your diet, your metabolism and overall digestive system improve. In addition, proteins are the main “building blocks” for blood vessels and cells and, therefore, they are vital for the body.
Soybeans help prevent the development of atherosclerosis, which, in turn, leads to heart attacks. Moreover, it was proved that soy fiber is able to remove cholesterol formations from the walls of arteries and blood vessels.
